
{"id":"63ed3c62-3c78-4a97-9b23-c89745d42d35","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009573,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if 'nodes' not in case or 'required_symbols' not in case:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing required fields')\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not all(k in node for k in ('id','parent','symbol','depth','removable')):\\n            raise ValueError('Invalid node fields')\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    active = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    removable_nodes = [node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['parent'] is not None]\\n    removable_nodes.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def compute_descendants(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        sub = {nid}\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            sub.update(compute_descendants(child_id))\\n        descendants[nid] = sub\\n        return sub\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        compute_descendants(node['id'])\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for cand_node in removable_nodes:\\n        nid = cand_node['id']\\n        if nid not in active:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in active for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        sub = descendants[nid]\\n        sub_active = [sid for sid in sub if sid in active]\\n        possible = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            sub_count = sum(1 for sid in sub_active if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if sub_count > 0 and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - sub_count <= 0:\\n                possible = False\\n                break\\n        if not possible:\\n            continue\\n        for sid in sub_active:\\n            active.remove(sid)\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(active)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement the solve(case) function based on the problem description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" (SE-PE-01). The input case has a payload with nodes list and required_symbols list. The goal: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes while preserving at least one node for each required symbol, and ensuring that we don't remove nodes that have retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, its children must also be removed unless they are retained for required symbols?). Actually, reading the mechanism: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained (because they are required or necessary), we cannot remove that parent? But the root is always required because it's not removable (removable: false) and likely is the root symbol \"root\". Required_symbols list: e.g., [\"root\", \"s1\"]. So root is required and not removable. The algorithm: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So depth-first? Typically, grammar-guided reduction (like Perses) processes nodes in depth order, and tries to remove nodes if possible. But we have a simple tree structure with depth and parent IDs. Need to define what \"removing\" a node means: we want to produce the set of retained nodes. We have to return removed_node_ids (in order of removal?) and retained_node_ids. The output schema: two arrays of strings. The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (alphabetically? likely by ID string) and removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (the removal sequence). The algorithm: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"grammar_valid\" is a contract but we don't have grammar rules explicitly. Possibly the tree itself is grammar-valid? Perhaps we assume the input tree is grammar-valid. The reduction must maintain grammar validity? The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we don't know the grammar. Possibly the tree is already valid, and we just need to ensure that we don't break the required symbols. Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children\" means if after removal, some child nodes would be orphaned? Actually, if we remove a node, its subtree is also removed unless some children are needed for required symbols. But the statement \"node with retained children\" suggests we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., not removed). In a tree removal scenario, removing a parent removes all children. So \"node with retained children\" would be a node that has children that we want to keep. So we must not remove such a node. So the algorithm: iterate nodes in some order (deeper first), for each node that is removable (removable=true) and not required (its symbol is not in required_symbols? but also root is not removable), check if removing it would still leave all required symbols present in the remaining tree. Also check that no retained node would become orphaned? Actually, if we remove a node, its children are also removed. So if any child is needed (because its symbol is required or it has children that are required), then we cannot remove the parent. This is like \"node with retained children\". So we need to consider the subtree. Approach: Build a map from node id to node. Also build children mapping (parent -> list of children). Then we need to decide which nodes to keep. The goal is to remove as many removable nodes as possible? Possibly maximize removal? The description \"Reduce a registered parse tree\" suggests we want to minimize the tree while preserving interestingness (required symbols). So we want to remove nodes if possible. The algorithm: For each removable node that is not required, in depth order (deepest first), check if removing it (i.e., eliminating its entire subtree) would still satisfy that all required symbols are present somewhere else (i.e., there is at least one node with that symbol retained). Also check that its removal doesn't cause any non-removable parent to be removed? Actually, removing a node only affects ancestors if we delete along the path? In tree reduction, when you delete a node, you also delete its subtree, but you keep its parent. However, if you delete a node that is the only child of a parent, the parent remains (but becomes leaf). That's fine. The restriction \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if a node has any child that is retained (i.e., not removed), you cannot remove that node. Therefore, we must process in such a way that we remove leaves first, then their parents if after removal of children they become safe to remove, etc. That's like the Perses algorithm: it processes nodes bottom-up (post-order) and tries to delete a subtree if it is deletable (i.e., removing that subtree preserves interestingness). The provided description: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" So that's bottom-up. Also \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" And \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we should implement a reduction algorithm:\n\n1. Build tree: map id->node, parent->children.\n2. Identify required symbols set.\n3. Determine initial retained set: all nodes that are not removable? Actually, initially all nodes are retained. We'll consider removal candidates.\n4. We need to decide for each node whether to remove it. We'll process nodes sorted by depth descending, and for same depth, maybe by ID as tiebreaker.\n5. For each node that is removable (removable == True) and not required (symbol not in required_symbols, but also root is not removable anyway), and its parent is not null (it has a parent? root has parent null). Also, the node must not have any retained children. But initially, children are retained. So we need to first remove leaves, then after removing a leaf, its parent might become a leaf with no retained children, and then we can consider removing it. So we need an iterative process: after each removal, update states.\n6. The check: if we remove the node (meaning we remove its entire subtree from consideration), would the set of symbols present in the remaining tree still cover all required symbols? The remaining tree is the set of nodes that are not removed (including ancestors and siblings). So we need to track, for each required symbol, whether there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed. Initially we can compute a count per symbol. When we remove a node (including its subtree), we decrement counts for symbols in that subtree. If after removal all required symbol counts remain >=1, then removal is allowed. Otherwise, not. This also ensures we don't remove the last node of a required symbol.\n7. Also check \"node with retained children\": if we have already removed some children, then the node may have no retained children. But we must not remove a node that still has retained children. So before removing a node, we need to ensure that all its children are already removed (or that we are removing the entire subtree anyway). Since we process bottom-up, children will be considered first. If we remove a child, that child's subtree is gone. So after processing children, a parent may have zero retained children. So we can check: for node, are there any children that are still retained (i.e., not removed)? If yes, skip.\n8. Root is not removable (removable: false) so we won't attempt.\n9. Also, the node's parent might be removed later? That's fine; we are removing nodes in order.\n\nImportant: The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we must not remove it. But note: required_symbols is a list of symbols, not node IDs. So there may be multiple nodes with the same required symbol. We cannot remove any node that has a required symbol? Actually, the condition \"required symbol\" means the node's symbol is required. It says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That implies we cannot remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But that seems too strict: there might be multiple nodes with the same required symbol; we could remove some of them as long as at least one remains. But the instruction says \"a required symbol\" – likely means a node that is the only representative? Actually, the statement \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The root is a specific node. \"A required symbol\" is ambiguous: it might mean a node whose symbol is required? But then the example shows required_symbols includes \"root\" and \"s1\". There are multiple nodes with symbol \"s1\". In the first fixture, nodes n1 (depth1) and n4 (depth2) have symbol \"s1\". Are both not removable? In the fixture, n1 has removable: false, n4 has removable: true. So n4 is removable but its symbol is \"s1\" which is required. So likely the interpretation is that we cannot remove a node if it is the only node providing a required symbol? Or we cannot remove a node whose symbol is required? But n4 is removable and its symbol is s1, so it would be removed if allowed? The fixture says n4 removable: true, so it's allowed to be removed as long as s1 remains present elsewhere (n1). So \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" must mean: never remove a node that is the only node with that required symbol. Or maybe it means never remove a node that is itself designated as a required symbol (i.e., the node's symbol is required)? That would conflict with the fixture. So better: it means never remove the last node that has a required symbol. The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" already covers that. So the phrase \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" might be a misstatement or shorthand for never remove a required-symbol node if it would cause the symbol to be missing. The algorithm I described with counts handles that.\n\nAlso \"Never remove a node with retained children\": This is exactly the condition that we must not remove a node that still has children that are retained (because they are part of the tree). So processing bottom-up ensures we only remove leaves.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node has no children? Then it's a leaf. If it's removable and not required, we can remove it if required symbols remain present. But we also need to ensure that its removal doesn't affect ancestors' ability to have children? Ancestors can exist without children.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\n- Parse case: nodes list. Required symbols list.\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Also for each node, store list of children (by checking parent field).\n- Build initial symbol count: for each node, count per symbol.\n- Determine list of removable node IDs (where removable == True) sorted by depth descending, then by ID (string) as tiebreaker (stable).\n- We'll maintain a set of removed node ids.\n- Also maintain a list removed_order to track removal sequence.\n- We need to simulate removal of a node: when we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree (including itself). So we need to remove all descendants. So we can compute the subtree nodes for each node (including itself) before any removal. Since the tree is static, we can precompute subtree sets for each node. But be careful: removing a parent will remove its children that are already removed? That's fine; we can just mark them removed.\n- We'll have a set of \"active\" nodes (not removed). Initially all nodes are active.\n- For each candidate node in the sorted list:\n   - Check if it is removable (already true from list) and if it is still active (not already removed). Also check if symbol is required: we need to check if after removal, the required symbols are still present. But if there are multiple nodes with the required symbol, we can remove one if others exist.\n   - Also check that node has no active children: i.e., for all children (from precomputed children list), they are not in active set (or they are already removed). Since we process bottom-up, children should have been considered earlier and possibly removed. So we can check `all(child_id not in active_set for child_id in children[node_id])`.\n   - If condition holds, we check if removing this node (including its entire subtree) would cause any required symbol count to drop to zero. So we need to compute the subtree symbols from nodes that are still active in that subtree. But note: some nodes in the subtree may already be removed (if we removed children earlier). So we need to consider only active nodes that are in the subtree. Actually, when we remove a node, we want to remove all active nodes in its subtree. So we should compute the set of active nodes in the subtree that are not already removed. If we remove the node, those will be removed. So we can precompute for each node the active descendant ids (including itself) that are active. But that changes as we remove nodes. Better to compute on the fly: get_subtree_active(node_id): traverse its descendant tree (children list) and filter active.\n   - But we also need to know which symbols would be lost: for each required symbol, count how many active nodes have that symbol in this subtree. If any required symbol’s count in subtree equals the total active count for that symbol (i.e., removing this subtree would remove all remaining nodes of that symbol), then we cannot remove.\n   - So we need to maintain symbol counts of active nodes globally. We can have a dictionary active_symbols_counts for all active nodes. When we remove a subtree, we decrement counts for each symbol for each node removed, and add to removed set.\n   - Alternatively, for each candidate, compute if removal would reduce any required symbol's count to zero. That is: for each symbol s in required_symbols, let global_count = active_counts[s]; let subtree_count = number of active nodes in subtree with symbol s. If subtree_count == global_count, then removal would remove all nodes with that symbol, so not allowed.\n   - Also, root must not be removed, but root is not removable, so fine.\n   - If allowed, we execute removal: for each node in subtree active set, remove from active set, decrement symbol counts, add to removed set in order (for removed_order, we add the root of subtree? Or we add all removed node ids? The spec says \"removed_node_ids\" should be actual removal order. Does it mean the IDs of nodes that were removed, in the order they were removed? For each removal decision, we remove a whole subtree. Should we list the root of that subtree as the removed ID, or every node in the subtree? The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" And \"removed_node_ids\" is an array of strings. The subroutine \"Tentatively remove a node\" suggests we remove a single node (and implicitly its children). So likely the removed_node_ids list should contain the IDs of the nodes that were removed as the primary deletion. So when we remove a subtree, we record the node ID (the one we decided to remove). That matches typical reduction algorithms (e.g., Perses removes a node and its subtree, and records that rule application). So we will add the node_id to removed_order list.\n   - After processing all candidates, the retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes still active. Sort these IDs (as strings) alphabetically.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted_retained}.\n\nPotential issue: The order of removal: we process deepest first. This ensures removal of leaves before parents. This seems correct.\n\nNow we need to ensure we don't violate \"grammar_valid\". Since we don't have grammar rules, we assume the initial tree is valid and removal preserves validity? Possibly any subtree removal is grammar-valid if the parent can exist without that child? In standard tree reduction, removing a child is allowed if the grammar allows deletion of that subtree (e.g., optional constructs). But we have no grammar. The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but doesn't provide grammar. I think we can ignore grammar validation because it's not part of the payload. The constraint \"grammar_valid\" is a registered property that we cannot enforce without grammar. So we assume that any removal is grammar-valid. Or maybe the tree itself encodes grammar validity via the parent-child relationships: each child is a valid expansion of its parent's grammar rule. Removal of a child is always grammar-valid (since we are just deleting a subtree, leaving the parent with fewer children). In many grammars, some children are mandatory. But since we don't have that info, we treat all children as optional for deletion. The description says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but also \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar; parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" So we are abstracted away from actual grammar. The only constraints are tree structure, removable flag, required symbols, and the rules given.\n\nThus the algorithm I described should be correct.\n\nCheck with the first fixture case. Let's mentally simulate:\n\nNodes: root (depth0, removable: false, symbol root) - root required.\nn1 (depth1, parent root, removable: false, symbol s1)\nn2 (depth1, parent root, removable: true, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth1, parent root, removable: false, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable: true, symbol s1)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, removable: false, symbol s2)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable: true, symbol s0)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1.\n\nInitial active: all. Counts: root:1, s1:2 (n1,n4), s2:2 (n2,n5), s0:2 (n3,n6).\n\nSorted candidates by depth descending (depth2 first), then by ID: n4, n6? But n4 depth2, n5 depth2 (but n5 removable false), n6 depth2. So candidates: n4 (depth2, rem true, id \"n4\"), n6 (depth2, rem true, id \"n6\"). Also n2 depth1, but depth1 after depth2.\n\nProcess n4: removable true, symbol s1 (required). Check children: n4 has no children? In data, n4 parent n2, but no children listed. So children list empty. So no active children condition satisfied. Check required symbols: global s1 count = 2, subtree of n4 includes only n4 (active subtree). So subtree s1 count = 1. Since global s1 count = 2, removal would leave 1 s1 (n1), so allowed. Therefore remove n4. Remove from active set, decrement s1 count to 1, add n4 to removed_order. Active set: remove n4.\n\nProcess n6: depth2, rem true, symbol s0, children none. s0 global count = 2 (n3,n6). subtree s0 count=1, so removal would leave 1 (n3). Allowed. Remove n6, s0 count becomes 1. removed_order: [n4, n6]. Active: remove n6.\n\nNow depth1 candidates: n2, n3? n3 removable false, ignore. n1 removable false. So only n2 (rem true, symbol s2). Check children: n2's children list originally [n4]. But n4 is already removed (not active). So no active children. subtree active of n2: includes n2 itself. s2 global count = 2 (n2 and n5). subtree s2 count = 1 (n2). Removal would leave n5 with s2, so allowed. Also check that n2 is not required symbol (s2 not in required). Remove n2. s2 count becomes 1. removed_order: [n4,n6,n2]. Active set remove n2.\n\nNow depth0 nothing else. Remaining active nodes: root, n1, n3, n5. IDs: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"]. removed: [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"]. That seems plausible. The required symbols root and s1 (n1) remain. This matches the expected behavior.\n\nNow we need to output the Python code as a JSON string inside an object with key \"implementation\". The code must define solve(case) that returns the JSON object with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nWe'll implement the algorithm exactly as described.\n\nPotential pitfalls:\n- The case payload structure: nodes list, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Utility is provided but not used in our algorithm.\n- depth field is given; we can use it for sorting. But we can also compute depth from tree traversal, but depth is provided.\n- The _case_nonce is extra, ignore.\n- The required_symbols list may include root (the symbol \"root\") or other symbols.\n- Node IDs may not be sorted; we need stable tiebreak by node ID string (lexicographic).\n- When sorting candidates, depth descending; for same depth, sort by ID ascending? The spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" No order direction specified. We'll assume ascending ID (since tiebreak, and typically alphabetical). That should be fine.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node is removable but has active children that are not required? Should not remove because children are retained. Our algorithm checks that no children are active. That's correct.\n\nWhat if a node has required symbol and there are multiple nodes with that symbol; we still should not remove it if it has children? The condition \"node with retained children\" takes precedence. If it has children, we cannot remove it regardless of required. So our algorithm will skip it because children are active.\n\nThe order of removal: we add the node ID to removed list when we decide to remove it. This matches the examples.\n\nNow code structure:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # parse\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children = {}  # id -> list of child ids\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node['id']\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\n        children.setdefault(node.get('parent'), []).append(nid)  # but root parent null, so we can store for null.\n\n    # Initialize active set and symbol counts\n    active = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n\n    # Determine removable and not required? Actually all removable nodes are candidates, but we may skip nodes that are required if they are the last, but we'll check during process.\n    # We'll collect all removable node ids, then sort by depth descending, then id.\n    removable_nodes = [node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['parent'] is not None]  # root parent None, but root not removable anyway.\n    # sort\n    removable_nodes.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n\n    # Precompute subtree sets for each node (static tree). Since tree doesn't change structure, we can compute descendants once.\n    descendants = {}  # node id -> set of ids in subtree including self\n    def compute_descendants(nid):\n        if nid in descendants:\n            return descendants[nid]\n        sub = {nid}\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\n            sub.update(compute_descendants(child_id))\n        descendants[nid] = sub\n        return sub\n    for node in nodes:\n        compute_descendants(node['id'])\n\n    removed_order = []\n\n    # Process each candidate\n    for cand_node in removable_nodes:\n        nid = cand_node['id']\n        if nid not in active:\n            continue\n        # Check if node has active children\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in active for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # Compute subtree active nodes that would be removed\n        sub = descendants[nid]  # all potential nodes\n        sub_active = [sid for sid in sub if sid in active]\n        # Check that for each required symbol, removing sub_active does not zero out count\n        possible = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            sub_count = sum(1 for sid in sub_active if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\n            if sub_count > 0 and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - sub_count <= 0:\n                # removal would eliminate last node of required symbol\n                possible = False\n                break\n        if not possible:\n            continue\n        # Perform removal\n        for sid in sub_active:\n            active.remove(sid)\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n\n    # After processing, retained nodes are those still active.\n    retained = sorted(active)\n    # But ensure root is retained (it should be)\n    return {\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\n    }\n\nCheck: root is not removable, so never in removable_nodes list. Good.\n\nPotential issue: When we remove a node, we remove all active nodes in its subtree. But suppose a child was already removed earlier because we processed it. Then sub_active will not include that child. That's fine. The count for symbol of that child was already decremented. So the subtree counts will only reflect the nodes that are still active. This ensures that when we later remove the parent, we don't subtract the child's symbol again. That's correct.\n\nEdge case: multiple nodes with same symbol; removal of a subtree may reduce count but not to zero. Our check ensures that only if sub_count == global_count for any required symbol we block. But sub_count is for active nodes in that subtree. However, it's possible that global count includes nodes that are also in other subtrees that will be removed later? That's fine because they are still active now. So we check per removal.\n\nNow, we should also consider \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\". We've encoded interestingness as required symbols presence. That seems correct.\n\nNow formatting the output: The envelope: whole response must be exactly one JSON object with single key \"implementation\", whose value is Python source as a JSON string (escaped). So we need to generate a JSON string that contains the code. We'll write the code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_by_id[nid] = node\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n    active = set(node['id'] for node in nodes)\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\\n    removable_nodes = [node for node in nodes if node['removable'] and node['parent'] is not None]\\n    removable_nodes.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def compute_descendants(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        sub = {nid}\\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\\n            sub.update(compute_descendants(child_id))\\n        descendants[nid] = sub\\n        return sub\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        compute_descendants(node['id'])\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for cand_node in removable_nodes:\\n        nid = cand_node['id']\\n        if nid not in active:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in active for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        sub = descendants[nid]\\n        sub_active = [sid for sid in sub if sid in active]\\n        possible = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            sub_count = sum(1 for sid in sub_active if node_by_id[sid]['symbol'] == sym)\\n            if sub_count > 0 and symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) - sub_count <= 0:\\n                possible = False\\n                break\\n        if not possible:\\n            continue\\n        for sid in sub_active:\\n            active.remove(sid)\\n            sym = node_by_id[sid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n    retained = sorted(active)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained\\n    }\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure the code is correct and uses no external imports. It uses only standard Python.\n\nDouble-check: The case payload includes utility field, which we ignore. That's fine.\n\nNow ensure we handle the case where root's parent is null. We added condition node['parent'] is not None for removable_nodes to exclude root. But what if there is a node with parent null that is not root? Probably not.\n\nAlso note: The fixture shows nodes list with each node having \"parent\": null for root. So we correctly avoid processing root.\n\nPotential issue: The children dict is built by iterating nodes and adding to parent's list. But we only added nodes with parent not None. That's fine.\n\nNow test the code mentally on the given fixture. It should produce removed_order: [\"n4\",\"n6\",\"n2\"] and retained: [\"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"] (alphabetically sorted: \"n1\",\"n3\",\"n5\",\"root\"). That matches expectation.\n\nEdge case: What if required_symbols includes a symbol that appears only on the root? Then removing any node with that symbol (root is not removable) is fine. If required symbol appears on a removable leaf, we may remove it only if another node with same symbol exists. The algorithm handles.\n\nNow wh